WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.500
INTRO:
Ah, Wells Fargo, the 3rd largest bank in the

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:09.650
US with a total of $1.78 trillion in assets
under management. Aside from being massive

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:16.090
in size, Wells Fargo also has a massive history
being founded nearly 200 years ago in 1852.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.410
Given their rich history and dominant position,
you would think that Wells Fargo is one of

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:25.520
the most respected and trusted financial institutions
in the world, but this couldn’t be further

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.570
from the truth. In fact, Yahoo ranked Wells
Fargo as the second most hated company in

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.740
the US in 2020, only beaten out by the Weinstein
company. For people who don’t bank with

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:41.070
Wells Fargo or follow the financial industry
too closely, this might be a bit surprising.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:46.060
But it just takes one glance at their history
to understand why they’re so despised. On

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.840
their Wikipedia page, for example, the Lawsuits,
fines, and controversies section is literally

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.790
longer than their history section. So, here’s
the controversial history of Wells Fargo and

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.960
why they’re so dominant despite all their
shortcomings.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:05.210
STARTING WITH A BAILOUT:
Taking a look back, the history of Wells Fargo

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:09.960
circles back to two men named Henry Wells
and William Fargo. These two were actually

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.229
co-founders of American Express, which was
at the time a pretty popular express company

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:18.970
in New York. William and Henry wanted to expand
American Express to California and leverage

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:24.180
the gold rush, but the board opposed this
decision. Reluctant to give up the idea, the

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.280
duo ended up starting Wells Fargo on March
18, 1852. Given their history with express

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.109
services, Wells Fargo was also naturally an
express company, but they also dabbled in

00:01:34.109 --> 00:01:39.399
banking as well. At first, things were looking
super bright as depositors flooded in throughout

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:44.030
the early 1850s, but it didn’t take long
to understand why Amex’s board refused to

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:49.329
expand to California. In 1855, news arrived
that the parent company of the bank Page,

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:53.619
Bacon, &amp; Company had collapsed due to over
speculation. This triggered a bank run which

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:58.310
quickly escalated into a statewide banking
panic. Within just a week, customers withdrew

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:03.240
so much money that Wells Fargo lost ⅓ of
their total assets. While this was disastrous

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.259
for banks that went under, and customers who
couldn’t retrieve their money, this actually

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:11.790
worked out quite well for Wells Fargo. You
see, Wells Fargo was one of the few banks

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:16.370
that survived the panic which gave them 2
major advantages. First of all, Wells Fargo

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:21.780
had very little remaining competition in California,
and secondly, surviving the panic gave Californians

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:27.120
much more trust and confidence in Wells Fargo.
I’m not sure if this trust was really deserved

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.470
though as it didn’t take long for Wells
Fargo to delve into a sketchy investment.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.520
Wells Fargo turned around and lent these new
deposits to a startup called Overland Mail

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.600
Company. The first red flag was the reasoning
behind the loan as it’s not clear if the

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.430
loan was made based on merit or nepotism.
You see, the founders of Overland Mail had

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.850
substantial stakes in Wells Fargo and American
Express. And the president of the company

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:54.700
was Amex’s third cofounder John Butterfield.
Anyway, things started off decent in 1858

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:59.340
as Overland had a contract from the US government,
but the problem was that this was their primary

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.459
source of revenue. And in 1859, when Congress
failed to pass the annual post office appropriation

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:08.420
bill, Overland received no funding which led
them to take out even more debt from Wells

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:13.629
Fargo. It didn’t take long for Overland
to become a default risk and in 1860, Wells

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:18.159
Fargo threatened to foreclose on Overland.
The founders of Overland didn’t resist much

00:03:18.159 --> 00:03:22.430
and they agreed to let Wells Fargo take over
the company, but this didn’t help much given

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:26.870
their dire situation. Another express company
that was founded around the same time and

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.450
facing a similar issue called Pony Express
would actually shut down just 6 months later.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:36.400
So, things were looking pretty sketchy, but
luckily for Wells Fargo, the US government

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.860
awarded Overland a $1 million express contract
in 1861. Wells Fargo hadn’t even been around

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:46.879
for 10 years yet, but somehow, they had already
received their first bailout.

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:51.760
SMOOTH SAILING:
Despite that early encounter with Overland,

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.170
the next century was pretty smooth for Wells
Fargo. Throughout the rest of the 1800s, Wells

00:03:56.170 --> 00:04:00.129
Fargo mainly focused on growing its express
business which proved to be quite lucrative.

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:04.989
In 1869, for example, Wells Fargo paid a massive
premium to acquire the Pacific Union Express

00:04:04.989 --> 00:04:09.840
Company which gave them exclusive rights to
the Central Pacific Railroad for 10 years.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.810
However, this deal also stipulated that the
leader of Pacific Union Lloyd Tevis would

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:18.419
takeover over Wells Fargo as president. William
Fargo felt that this was a beneficial trade

00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:20.239
for the company long-term, and in 1872, he
would resign as president and return to American

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:24.810
Express. Under Lloyd’s leadership, the banking
and express offices grew from 436 in 1871

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:30.030
to 3500 by the end of the century. Wells Fargo
also established America’s first transcontinental

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:34.080
express line which leveraged over a dozen
railroads. This allowed them to expand into

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.699
the lucrative northeast market, but Lloyd
wasn’t satisfied with just that. He also

00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:43.780
expanded the express service internationally
into Japan, Australia, Hong Kong, South America,

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:48.280
Mexico, and Europe. On top of this, Lloyd
also expanded into the money order business

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.870
and Wells Fargo marched into the 1900s full
force. But the early 1900s wasn’t that pleasant

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:59.180
for Wells Fargo due to external circumstances.
In 1906, San Francisco was hit with an earthquake

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:03.539
that destroyed Wells Fargo’s national bank
building. The bank’s vault did survive but

00:05:03.539 --> 00:05:07.810
Wells Fargo was forced to spend significant
resources on building back. On the bright

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:11.580
side, citizens and other businesses also spent
significant money on their own properties

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.360
which led to a massive influx of capital.
Within the next 18 months, Wells Fargo’s

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:21.310
deposits increased from $16 million to $35
million. Unfortunately, though, this didn’t

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:26.699
last long as another banking panic came around
in 1907. Wells Fargo didn’t have any liquidity

00:05:26.699 --> 00:05:30.770
problems, but the panic was quite painful
as they lost $1 million worth of deposits

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:34.740
every week for 6 weeks straight. This was
nothing though compared to what they would

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:39.819
lose in 1918. Due to World War 1, President
Wilson would approve the nationalization of

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:43.770
all express companies, so Wells Fargo was
forced to give up their express business.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:47.780
Now, you might be thinking that there were
more losses to come given that the Great Depression

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:53.370
was just around the corner. But this was actually
not the case. The president of Wells Fargo

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:57.660
at the time, Frederick Lipman, was an extremely
conservative investor. He chose to reinvest

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:02.210
all the bank’s profits into trusts as opposed
to the stock market. So, Wells Fargo didn’t

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:06.919
even feel the Great Depression. In fact, Wells
Fargo helped bail out failing institutions

00:06:06.919 --> 00:06:11.940
and their deposits actually grew $2 million
during the Banking Holiday of 1933. This stellar

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.800
performance earned Wells Fargo unprecedented
amounts of trust and respect. Wells Fargo

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.940
used all this new interest to make loads of
financial acquisitions, and by the 1970s,

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:25.000
they emerged as one of the most dominant forces
within the banking industry. Unfortunately,

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:31.620
though, it didn’t take long for employees
and executives to start abusing this trust.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:36.509
HOLE IN THE REPUTATION:
In January of 1981, Judith MacLardie was just

00:06:36.509 --> 00:06:41.319
completing one of her routine audients of
Wells Fargo when she uncovered a $21.3 million

00:06:41.319 --> 00:06:46.460
hole in the records. At first, most of the
banking community refused to believe it. This

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.890
must’ve been some sort of error or mistake.
There’s no way Wells Fargo was linked to

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:55.320
any sort of fraud. Just three months before
this, a top auditing official named Edward

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:59.460
Croak proudly announced that Wells Fargo was
in an enviable position thanks to their iron

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:05.350
fist on fraud and embezzlement. Yet, here
they were. It turns out that between 1978

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:09.720
and 1981, an employee named Lloyd Benjamin
Lewis had been writing fake debit and credit

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:14.449
receipts to Sam Marshall who was the president
of Muhammed Ali Professional Sports. Lloyd

00:07:14.449 --> 00:07:20.330
had used this tactic to funnel out $21.3 million
out of which he received $300,000. Lloyd ended

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:25.260
up pleading guilty and testifying against
his co-conspirators. No executives were charged,

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:29.340
but it is a bit suspicious that it took so
long to catch the fraud. It’s not like Lloyd

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:33.819
was carrying out a very sophisticated fraud.
Something that we should note though is that

00:07:33.819 --> 00:07:39.539
the CEO at the time Richard Cooley did resign
shortly after in 1982 despite not being charged.

00:07:39.539 --> 00:07:44.210
This poked the first holes into Wells Fargo’s
reputation in a hundred years, and Wells Fargo’s

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:48.199
deposits started to stagnate for the first
time in decades. In order to accommodate for

00:07:48.199 --> 00:07:53.610
this slower growth, Wells Fargo placed their
emphasis on acquisitions once again. In 1986,

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:58.770
Wells Fargo purchased Crocker National Bank
for $1.1 billion, and in 1988, the purchased

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:04.319
Barclays Bank of California for $125 million.
Moving into the 1990s, they considered a merger

00:08:04.319 --> 00:08:09.580
with American Express, but this fell through
after the executives had a disagreement. Nonetheless,

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:13.939
Wells Fargo continued on in their acquisitions
spree. In 1996, they purchased Interstate

00:08:13.939 --> 00:08:19.870
Bancorp for $11.6 billion, and in 1998, they
merged with Norwest. All these acquisitions

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:24.069
allowed Wells Fargo to continue their rapid
growth throughout the 80s and 90s, but there

00:08:24.069 --> 00:08:28.349
was no way they were going to buy their way
out of the dot-com crash. So, like the rest

00:08:28.349 --> 00:08:31.830
of the banking industry, they tried to loan
their way out and that brings us into the

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:33.479
2008 financial crisis.

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:38.910
FRAUD AFTER FRAUD:
The 2008 financial recession really needs

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:43.690
no introduction as it was by no means specific
to Wells Fargo. A large number of banks got

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:48.330
involved in risky subprime mortgage lending
which eventually came to bite them back hard.

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:52.110
As borrowers began to default, massive banks
like Lehman Brothers and Bear Sterns were

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:56.680
taken down completely. After this, the government
panicked and decided to bail out the remaining

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:01.080
banks which has since become one of the most
controversial decisions in history. Wells

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:05.390
Fargo received a $25 billion bailout, and
this permanently tarnished the reputation

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:10.610
of the bank. The silver lining was that competitors
like JP Morgan Chase and Bank of America also

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:15.570
received similar bailouts, so the public outrage
wasn’t just targetted at Wells Fargo. At

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:19.680
this point, it would’ve been best if Wells
Fargo just stayed out of the spotlight and

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.220
played it safe, but instead, they decided
to stage an ambitious recovery. The executives

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:28.340
started putting out impossible sales goals
and ramped up pressure to the max. They had

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:32.839
lost decades of progress throughout the recession,
and they were determined to regain their losses.

00:09:32.839 --> 00:09:36.970
But the problem was that the sales goals were
literally impossible, and desperate to meet

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:41.430
the sales goals, many employees started to
fake numbers. Over the next several years,

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:44.930
employees opened up 1.5 million checking and
savings accounts under their customers’

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:50.640
names without their consent. They also opened
up 500,000 credit cards again without customers’

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.480
consent. When this scandal was finally exposed
in September of 2016, Wells Fargo lost all

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:00.380
credibility. And I don’t just mean figuratively,
Wells Fargo literally lost accreditation with

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:05.990
the Better Business Bureau. On top of this,
they were fined $185 million for the fiasco,

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:10.519
but this doesn’t really mean much given
that they profit roughly $20 billion per year.

00:10:10.519 --> 00:10:14.889
Wells Fargo may have escaped serious financial
consequences, but in terms of reputation,

00:10:14.889 --> 00:10:18.870
this was the final straw. Applications for
credit cards and checking accounts fell off

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:24.040
a cliff, and total assets have stagnated ever
since. This might not sound that bad at first

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:28.070
glance, but when you consider the rampant
inflation and the parabolic moves within real

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:33.240
estate and the stock market, this is terrible.
Chase and Bank of America aren’t really

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:38.820
liked either, but both of them are up about
50% in assets since 2016. So, really, Wells

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:42.140
Fargo is not stagnating, they’re actually
declining.

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:47.800
WELLS FARGO TODAY:
To make things worse, everything we discussed

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:52.750
so far was just the biggest scandals and frauds.
Wells Fargo has also had plenty of smaller

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:56.960
schemes as well. Over the years, they’ve
been accused of ignoring money laundering

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.230
schemes, discriminating against African American
and Hispanic borrowers, violating credit card

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:05.980
laws, committing insider trading, lobbying,
and rolling out predatory lending programs.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:09.480
Considering all this, I don’t think anyone
is surprised by Wells Fargo’s high ranking

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:13.481
on the most hated list. And that’s really
a shame given that Wells Fargo was the heroes

00:11:13.481 --> 00:11:16.410
of the great depression, but nearly all of
it was self-inflicted. Looking forward, Wells

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:21.190
Fargo may not have much longer. Almost everyone
agrees that given the ludicrous debt levels,

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:26.680
a great deleveraging is inevitable. Ray Dalio
has been preaching this for years, and recently,

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:31.290
even Elon Musk jumped on board. And given
that Wells Fargo is already on a decline,

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:35.860
they may get wiped out by this upcoming storm
just like Lehman Brothers. Who knows though,

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:40.000
Wells Fargo crushed the Great Depression,
so maybe they’ll surprise everyone and crush

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:44.649
the great deleveraging as well, but we’ll
just have to wait and see. Do you guys think

00:11:44.649 --> 00:11:49.120
Wells Fargo will crush the great deleveraging
or get crushed themselves? Comment that down

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:53.801
below. Also, drop a like if you’re not a
fan of big banks. And of course, consider

00:11:53.801 --> 00:11:57.351
joining our discord community to suggest future
video ideas and consider subscribing to see

00:11:57.351 --> 00:12:02.259
more questions logically answered.

